.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KVM-SNAP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kvm-snap
.Nd snapshot a virtual machine's disk, roll it back, list and delete snapshots
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Cm snap
.Ar name
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Cm rollback
.Op Ar snapshot
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Cm delete
.Ar snapshot
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages ZFS snapshots of a virtual machine's disk,
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar vm .
A snapshot takes no time and no space when it is taken; it grows only as
the disk moves away from it.
It is how a machine is put back to the moment before an upgrade, and what
.Xr kvm-clone 8
makes new machines from.
.Pp
Taking a snapshot of a running machine pauses it for the moment the
snapshot takes, so the disk is consistent, and resumes it whether the
snapshot succeeded or not.
.Pp
Rolling back powers the machine off, returns its disk to the snapshot,
destroys every snapshot newer than it (a ZFS rollback cannot jump over
them, and
.Nm
names each one first), and leaves the machine running or stopped as it
found it.
A paused machine is left stopped.
.Pp
A
.Ar snapshot
may be written three ways, all meaning the same:
.Ql stable ,
.Ql @stable
or
.Ql rpool/vms/ Ns Ar vm Ns Ql @stable .
A full name that is not a snapshot of
.Ar vm Ns 's
disk is refused, so a mistyped name can never roll back another machine.
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root; run as a user it re-executes itself with
.Xr sudo 8 .
.Fl -help
needs neither.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "rollback [snapshot]"
.It (no action)
Take a snapshot named for the current time,
.Ar YYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS .
.It Cm snap Ar name
Take a snapshot called
.Ar name :
letters, digits,
.Ql - ,
.Ql _ ,
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql \&: ,
up to 128 characters, not starting with
.Ql - .
A name that exists is refused.
.It Cm list
List the machine's snapshots with their creation time and the space each
holds on its own.
.It Cm rollback Op Ar snapshot
Roll back to
.Ar snapshot ,
or to the newest snapshot without one.
.It Cm delete Ar snapshot
Destroy
.Ar snapshot .
ZFS refuses while a clone of it exists.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
Needs no root and changes nothing.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar vm Ns Pa @ Ns Ar name
Each snapshot.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
The machine or the snapshot does not exist, a name is taken, a snapshot
belongs to another machine, or ZFS or libvirt refused.
.It 2
A usage error: no machine, a name that is not a plain name, a bad snapshot
name, an unknown action, or
.Cm delete
without a snapshot.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload host on 2026-09-29, against a throwaway machine,
.Ql sn-3208047 ,
made for the purpose with
.Xr kvm-create 8
and deleted afterwards.
.Ss Snapshot now (no action)
The machine was running, so it was paused for the moment:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap sn-3208047
Pausing sn-3208047 for a consistent snapshot...
Snapshot: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@2026-09-29_223451
.Ed
.Ss A named snapshot (snap)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 snap stable
Pausing sn-3208047 for a consistent snapshot...
Snapshot: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 snap stable
kvm-snap: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable already exists
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss What there is (list)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 list
Snapshots for sn-3208047:
NAME                                    CREATION                USED
rpool/vms/sn-3208047@2026-09-29_223451  Tue Sep 29 22:34 2026     0B
rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable             Tue Sep 29 22:34 2026     0B
rpool/vms/sn-3208047@later-1            Tue Sep 29 22:34 2026     0B
.Ed
.Ss Back to a snapshot (rollback)
The machine was running; the snapshot taken after
.Ql stable
is destroyed, and the machine is running again afterwards:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 rollback @stable
Rolling back to: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable
  destroying 1 newer snapshot(s):
    rpool/vms/sn-3208047@later-1
Rolled back to rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable (sn-3208047 is running)
.Ed
.Pp
A stopped machine stays stopped:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# (sn-3208047 powered off with virsh destroy)
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 rollback stable
Rolling back to: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable
Rolled back to rpool/vms/sn-3208047@stable (sn-3208047 is shut off)
.Ed
.Pp
Before an upgrade, with a way back:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap web01 snap pre-upgrade
# ...upgrade web01; if it goes wrong:
# kvm-snap web01 rollback pre-upgrade
.Ed
.Ss Deleting (delete)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 delete @del-a
Deleted: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@del-a
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 delete del-b
Deleted: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@del-b
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 delete rpool/vms/sn-3208047@del-c
Deleted: rpool/vms/sn-3208047@del-c
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 rollback @nosuch
kvm-snap: no snapshot rpool/vms/sn-3208047@nosuch
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 rollback rpool/vms/klab-golden-centos@golden
kvm-snap: rpool/vms/klab-golden-centos@golden is not a snapshot of rpool/vms/sn-3208047
# kvm-snap sn-3208047-empty rollback
kvm-snap: sn-3208047-empty has no snapshots
# kvm-snap nosuchvm
kvm-snap: no volume rpool/vms/nosuchvm
# (each of the four above exited 1)
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 snap 'bad name'
kvm-snap: 'bad name' is not a snapshot name (letters, digits, - _ . :; not starting with -)
# kvm-snap web/1
kvm-snap: 'web/1' is not a plain name (letters, digits, . _ -)
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 delete
kvm-snap: delete needs a snapshot name
\&...
# kvm-snap sn-3208047 frobnicate
kvm-snap: unknown action: frobnicate
\&...
# (each of the four above exited 2; the last two also print the usage)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kvm-snap: could not snapshot rpool/vms/VM"
ZFS refused the snapshot (the pool is full, or read-only).
A running machine was paused for it and has been resumed.
.It "kvm-snap: WARNING: VM is still PAUSED -- virsh resume VM"
Resuming failed; run the command it names.
.It "kvm-snap: WARNING: could not pause VM; the snapshot is taken live (crash-consistent)"
The snapshot is of a running disk: as consistent as after a power cut.
.It "kvm-snap: SNAPSHOT is not a snapshot of rpool/vms/VM"
A full snapshot name belonging to another dataset; nothing was changed.
.It "kvm-snap: zfs rollback -r SNAPSHOT failed; VM's disk is as it was"
The rollback did not happen, and the machine was started again if it had
been running.
.It "kvm-snap: rolled back, but VM did not start again"
The disk is at the snapshot; start the machine by hand to see why it does
not start.
.It "kvm-snap: could not delete SNAPSHOT"
Usually a clone made from it; ZFS names it above this line.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kld 1 ,
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr kldload-vm-snapshot 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr zfs-rollback 8 ,
.Xr zfs-snapshot 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-26 a rollback to an older snapshot was found to destroy the
running machine before
.Xr zfs-rollback 8
refused (the hourly
.Xr kldload-vm-snapshot 8
timer puts newer snapshots on every machine); rollback has used
.Fl r ,
and named what it destroys, since.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page:
.Cm rollback
and
.Cm delete
had accepted only a full snapshot name, while
.Xr kld 1
passes
.Ql @name ,
so every rollback and delete from kld's Snapshots tab had failed \(em and
this page's own delete example with it;
a full name belonging to another machine was accepted;
a failed snapshot left a running machine paused;
and a rollback always started the machine afterwards.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Rolling back destroys every snapshot newer than the target, including the
hourly ones
.Xr kldload-vm-snapshot 8
takes; that is ZFS's rule, not a choice.
To keep a newer state, clone it first with
.Xr kvm-clone 8 .
.Pp
The machine is powered off, not shut down, for a rollback: whatever it had
not written to disk is lost, which is what rolling back means.
.Pp
A pause for a snapshot is short but real: a latency-sensitive guest sees a
stall of the time
.Xr zfs-snapshot 8
takes.
